{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K7AL2fpPZiLZyhDeHBuaaJ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper and prior reviews. Let me compose the R2 review focused on novelty and related work.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure facets (precision, hardware) with methodology facets (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and measurement (item) across 5 benchmarks, 6 models, and 4M+ records. The three claimed contributions are: (1) a cross-benchmark variance gradient where item dominance drops monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), (2) a confirmatory separability null for infrastructure x methodology interactions (<0.3% under binary scoring), and (3) benchmark-specific D-study reproducibility budgets revealing that current practice yields G<0.25 everywhere. The paper also includes a fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa-large and a 14B scale validation.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The cross-benchmark variance gradient is the paper's genuine novel contribution and it is well-executed.** No prior work has positioned multiple benchmarks along a reasoning-intensity continuum and demonstrated a monotonic shift in variance structure. The 35pp item_id gradient (MMLU 56% to MATH 21%) with non-overlapping bootstrap CIs is statistically rigorous. More importantly, the \"unifying explanation\" framing -- that prior single-benchmark studies (Sclar on prompts, Pezeshkpour on ordering, Yuan on precision) reached seemingly contradictory conclusions because they studied benchmarks at different positions on the gradient -- is genuinely insightful and advances the field's understanding.\n\n2. **The facet effectiveness ratio inversion is non-obvious and actionable.** The finding that MMLU is item-limited (items:seeds = 14.2:1) while GSM8K is seed-limited (0.8:1) is not something a practitioner would predict a priori. This directly challenges the common assumption that evaluation protocols can be standardized across benchmarks. The D-study projection grids (Tables 9-10) are the most practically useful output of the paper.\n\n3. **Honest positioning of contributions.** The paper correctly frames the separability null as confirmatory rather than as a novelty claim, acknowledges that item dominance is expected from measurement theory (Brennan 2001), and explicitly scopes the infrastructure facet to numerical precision. This intellectual honesty is uncommon and appreciated. The engine confound discovery (vLLM V0 vs V1 routing) and its correction is a standalone methodological contribution that will save other researchers from the same pitfall.\n\n4. **Scale and thoroughness of the empirical study.** 4M+ evaluation records, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, 4 methodology facets, 2 infrastructure facets, per-model breakdowns, item resampling stability, REML sensitivity checks, bootstrap CIs, and 14B scale validation. This is a substantially more comprehensive study than Mesting (2026, single benchmark) or any prior isolated-factor study.\n\n5. **The G<0.25 finding is an important indictment.** Framing current standard practice (1 prompt, 1 seed, <=200 items) as producing measurement reliability below the minimum threshold accepted in any measurement discipline is alarming and should motivate the community.\n\n6. **Clear three-claim structure with well-differentiated contributions.** Each claim maps to specific tables and sections, making the paper's logic easy to follow despite its density.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The infrastructure crossing -- the claimed methodological advance over Mesting (2026) -- yields near-null results, making the paper's novelty narrative somewhat hollow (Section 6, p.6-7).** The paper's introduction frames \"crossing infrastructure with methodology in a unified G-theory framework\" as a key gap filled. But the infrastructure x methodology interaction is <0.3%, meaning this crossing contributes essentially nothing to our understanding. The paper's three genuine findings -- the gradient, the D-study reversal, and the G<0.25 indictment -- would all emerge from a Mesting-style methodology-only design replicated across 5 benchmarks. This raises the question: is the infrastructure crossing a genuine contribution or an expensive null that consumed compute without advancing knowledge? The paper should forthrightly address this in the Discussion. The confirmatory null has value (it validates isolated-factor studies), but the paper currently oversells it relative to its import.\n\n2. **Missing critical related work weakens the positioning (Section 2).** Several directly relevant papers are absent:\n   - **Brittlebench (2026)**: Reports ~50% variance from prompt perturbations with a perturbation protocol. Directly relevant to the prompt facet and omitted from text (only Mizrahi/Sclar cited for prompt sensitivity).\n   - **ThinkingMachines (2025)**: Documents 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention kernels. Directly relevant to the seed x item interaction at T=0 and to the vLLM infrastructure discussion. This reference would strengthen the mechanistic explanation for why seed effects are non-zero even at T=0.\n   - **Messina & Scotta (2026)**: The \"background temperature\" formalization. Listed in the bibliography but cited only tangentially in the paper_framing document; the actual paper text cites them once without engaging with the T_bg framework.\n   - **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)**: Alternative variance decomposition (intent/articulation/model uncertainty). Relevant as a competing framework that would enrich the Related Work discussion.\n   - **GeneralizIT (2024)**: The Python G-theory software. If used, should be cited; if not, should be discussed as a tooling reference.\n   - **Bui et al. (2025)**: Seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22). Directly relevant to the fine-tuning G-study in Section 8.2.\n\n3. **The \"first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis\" claim needs careful qualification (Section 1, Contributions).** Bouthillier et al. (2021) performed ANOVA-based variance decomposition across multiple ML benchmarks (ImageNet, CIFAR, etc.) with multiple facets. The paper positions this as \"ANOVA, not G-theory\" -- technically correct, but ANOVA and G-theory share the same variance decomposition; the difference is D-study projections. Mesting (2026) applied G-theory to LLM evaluation on one benchmark. The paper's claim is thus \"first G-theory (not ANOVA) applied to LLM evaluation (not ML generally) across multiple benchmarks.\" This is a narrow novelty window that the paper should state more precisely. A reviewer less charitable than me would call this incremental.\n\n4. **The three-tier benchmark clustering (knowledge / commonsense / reasoning) is post-hoc and lacks external validation (Section 5).** The gradient is presented as a monotonic continuum, but the benchmark ordering (MMLU > ARC > HellaSwag > GSM8K > MATH) was chosen by the authors, presumably after observing the results. An independent measure of \"reasoning intensity\" -- such as average chain-of-thought length, number of reasoning steps, or an IRT-derived difficulty parameter -- would make the continuum principled rather than constructed. Without this, the gradient could be an artifact of the specific 5 benchmarks selected, and a 6th benchmark might not fall on the line.\n\n5. **Comparison with IRT-based approaches is superficial (Section 2, paragraph 4).** The paper claims D-study budgets are \"directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling recommendations\" (Polo et al., tinyBenchmarks) but never actually compares them. What does tinyBenchmarks recommend for MMLU -- and how does it differ from the G-theory prescription of 420 items? If they agree, G-theory is confirmatory; if they disagree, the paper must explain why. This is a missed opportunity to demonstrate G-theory's added value over an established, widely-adopted approach.\n\n6. **Zero figures is a critical presentation failure for a variance decomposition paper.** 15-19 tables and no figures. Stacked-bar variance decomposition charts are the expected field idiom (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026). The cross-benchmark gradient practically demands a visual: the 35pp monotonic decline would be immediately legible in a stacked-bar chart ordered by benchmark. D-study projection curves (G vs. facet sample size) would communicate the protocol reversal far more effectively than projection grids in tables. At a top venue with 25% acceptance rate, this is a significant competitive disadvantage.\n\n7. **The fine-tuning G-study (Section 8.2) feels architecturally disjoint.** RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA is methodologically distant from 7-14B decoder inference-time evaluation. The facets differ (learning rate and data shuffle replace prompt template and temperature), the architecture differs (encoder-only vs. decoder), and the paradigm differs (training vs. inference). The finding that \"item_id is rank-1 in fine-tuning too\" is interesting but not surprising -- it would be surprising if item difficulty did not dominate in any measurement scenario. The cross-paradigm generalization claimed is weak and should be explicitly qualified as preliminary evidence, not as a contribution on par with the main findings.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gradient robustness**: If you added a 6th benchmark (e.g., GPQA, TruthfulQA, or HumanEval), would it fall on the monotonic gradient line? What is your prediction, and what would it mean for the gradient claim if it did not?\n\n2. **Gradient mechanism**: You attribute the shift to chain-of-thought stochasticity, but HellaSwag (commonsense completion) does not typically involve explicit CoT. What mechanism drives the gradient for HellaSwag specifically?\n\n3. **IRT comparison**: For MMLU specifically, Polo et al. (2024) recommend ~100 items via IRT-based subsampling. Your D-study recommends 420 items for G>=0.90. Can you explain this 4x discrepancy? Are these answering different questions?\n\n4. **Judge variance interaction**: Mesting (2026) found judge identity dominates at 43.8%. Your entire variance structure sits \"underneath\" this. If a practitioner uses LLM-as-judge evaluation, do your D-study budgets still apply, or are they overwhelmed by judge variance?\n\n5. **Incremental over Mesting**: If Mesting had replicated their study on 5 benchmarks instead of 1, they would have discovered the same gradient (since infrastructure contributes <0.3%). What does the infrastructure crossing add beyond a confirmatory null?\n\n6. **Counter-intuitive G pattern**: You note that knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks (MMLU 0.36 vs GSM8K 0.55). Is this because the G coefficient is dominated by item sampling error on MMLU? If so, this is a feature of G-theory's definition rather than a property of the benchmarks themselves. Can you disentangle?\n\n7. **Negative variance estimates**: How are Henderson III negative estimates handled for near-zero components? This directly affects the separability claim.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography contains 22 references. The paper_framing document targets 25+. Several of the missing references listed above would bring this closer to adequate coverage for a venue submission.\n\n2. Vania et al. (2021) appears in the bibliography but is not discussed in the Related Work text.\n\n3. The title includes \"Evaluation Metrics\" (plural) but the main analysis uses only binary correct scoring. The logprob analysis is a single paragraph in Discussion. Either deliver the metric-conditional analysis promised by the title or revise the title.\n\n4. Table density: 15+ tables in 10 pages is extremely dense. Several tables (e.g., Tables 3 and 4 on per-model breakdowns) could be consolidated or moved to an appendix without losing main-text narrative.\n\n5. The claim \"over 4 million evaluation records\" in the abstract should be verified against the sum of reported records: MMLU (1,036,800) + cross-benchmark (1,382,400) + hardware (691,200) + 14B (345,600) + fine-tuning (records unclear) + MATH (460,800) + LayerCast (172,800) = ~4.1M. The accounting is plausible but should be presented explicitly.\n\n6. Section 3.3 mentions \"V0 engine in enforce_eager mode\" without defining these vLLM-specific terms for readers unfamiliar with vLLM internals.\n\n### Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Rationale**: The cross-benchmark variance gradient is a genuine empirical contribution that advances the field's understanding of LLM evaluation variance. The D-study budgets are practically useful and the G<0.25 finding is important. However, the paper's claimed methodological advance over Mesting (infrastructure crossing) yields near-null results, meaning the novelty rests primarily on replicating a known methodology across more benchmarks and discovering an empirical regularity -- valuable, but not a methodological contribution. Missing related work, zero figures, a somewhat post-hoc gradient construction, and the absence of a direct IRT comparison weaken the presentation. With figures added, missing references incorporated, and the gradient externally validated, this would move to a clear 5/6. As submitted, it sits at the acceptance threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4f148f2fd0307ae6 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4f148f2fd0307ae6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 58910\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 149194</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a1cd9cb-5673-4b3d-b760-963c998bdaf6","uuid":"14bf343c-800c-4315-a65e-32b45ca11131","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:43:24.566Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Review standard: top ML venue, acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure (numerical precision, hardware) with methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and measurement (item selection) facets across 5 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag, MATH), 4 7-9B models + 2 14B models, and 2 GPU types, totaling 3M+ observations. Three claims: (1) item dominance is benchmark-conditional (48% knowledge → 28% proof reasoning) forming a 3-tier clustering; (2) infra×method separability holds on knowledge benchmarks (<0.5%) but breaks on math-generation (3%); (3) reproducibility budgets are metric-dependent (G=0.37 binary vs G=0.97 text-match).\n\n## Paper Structure (10 pages)\n- Pages 1-2: Abstract, Intro, Related Work (4 subsections: variance sources, G-theory in NLP, item-level/efficient benchmarking, training instability)\n- Page 3: Framework (G-theory basics with Eq 1-2, Experimental Design listing 9 experiments)\n- Page 4-8: Results (12 subsections with 15 tables)\n- Page 9: Discussion (5 paragraphs + Limitations), Conclusion\n- Page 10: References (15 entries)\n\n## Related Work as Presented\nThe paper cites:\n- Yuan et al. (2025): Up to 9% accuracy from precision — paper extends with item-level G-theory breakdown\n- Mizrahi et al. (2024, Brittlebench): ~50% variance from prompts — paper crosses prompts with infrastructure\n- Lu et al. (2022), Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024): Seed/ordering effects — paper includes both as facets\n- Biderman et al. (2024): Reproducibility lessons — positioned as motivation\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021): ANOVA for ML benchmarks — paper extends to G-theory\n- Mesting (2026): G-theory for LLM eval, 5 methodology facets, single benchmark (MMLU) — paper adds infrastructure crossing + multi-benchmark\n- IRT: Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), Vania et al. (2021), Polo et al. (2024, tinyBenchmarks), Perlitz et al. (2023) — positioned as complementary\n- Training: Dodge et al. (2020), Sellam et al. (2022, MultiBERTs), Henderson et al. (2018), Dehghani et al. (2021, Benchmark Lottery) — fine-tuning G-study extends this\n\n## Key Claims to Assess for Novelty\n1. \"First multi-benchmark G-theory analysis of LLM evaluation\" — Is this truly novel vs. Mesting (2026) and Bouthillier et al. (2021)?\n2. \"Infrastructure×methodology crossing in unified design\" — Has this been done before?\n3. \"Task-type clustering / benchmark-conditional variance structure\" — How novel vs. existing benchmark comparison work?\n4. \"Model-as-facet revealing 16-22% model×item\" — Standard in G-theory but novel in LLM context?\n5. \"Metric-dependent reproducibility budgets via D-study\" — Novel contribution or straightforward application?\n6. \"Fine-tuning variance decomposition with G-theory\" — Novel vs. Dodge et al., Sellam et al.?\n\n## Your Review Focus Areas\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the contribution primarily methodological (applying G-theory to new setting), empirical (new findings), or both? Is this sufficient for a top venue?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Contribution clarity**: Are the three claimed contributions well-differentiated from prior work?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the \"benchmark-conditional variance structure\" framing compelling? Is the three-tier clustering well-motivated or post-hoc?\n5. **Broader impact**: Does the paper advance the field meaningfully? Will practitioners actually use D-study projections?\n6. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-organized for its density (15 tables in 10 pages)? Is the narrative coherent?\n\n## Output Format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, be specific with page/section references]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a4f148f2fd0307ae6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper and prior reviews. Let me compose the R2 review focused on novelty and related work.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure facets (precision, hardware) with methodology facets (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and measurement (item) across 5 benchmarks, 6 models, and 4M+ records. The three claimed contributions are: (1) a cross-benchmark variance gradient where item dominance drops monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), (2) a confirmatory separability null for infrastructure x methodology interactions (<0.3% under binary scoring), and (3) benchmark-specific D-study reproducibility budgets revealing that current practice yields G<0.25 everywhere. The paper also includes a fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa-large and a 14B scale validation.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The cross-benchmark variance gradient is the paper's genuine novel contribution and it is well-executed.** No prior work has positioned multiple benchmarks along a reasoning-intensity continuum and demonstrated a monotonic shift in variance structure. The 35pp item_id gradient (MMLU 56% to MATH 21%) with non-overlapping bootstrap CIs is statistically rigorous. More importantly, the \"unifying explanation\" framing -- that prior single-benchmark studies (Sclar on prompts, Pezeshkpour on ordering, Yuan on precision) reached seemingly contradictory conclusions because they studied benchmarks at different positions on the gradient -- is genuinely insightful and advances the field's understanding.\n\n2. **The facet effectiveness ratio inversion is non-obvious and actionable.** The finding that MMLU is item-limited (items:seeds = 14.2:1) while GSM8K is seed-limited (0.8:1) is not something a practitioner would predict a priori. This directly challenges the common assumption that evaluation protocols can be standardized across benchmarks. The D-study projection grids (Tables 9-10) are the most practically useful output of the paper.\n\n3. **Honest positioning of contributions.** The paper correctly frames the separability null as confirmatory rather than as a novelty claim, acknowledges that item dominance is expected from measurement theory (Brennan 2001), and explicitly scopes the infrastructure facet to numerical precision. This intellectual honesty is uncommon and appreciated. The engine confound discovery (vLLM V0 vs V1 routing) and its correction is a standalone methodological contribution that will save other researchers from the same pitfall.\n\n4. **Scale and thoroughness of the empirical study.** 4M+ evaluation records, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, 4 methodology facets, 2 infrastructure facets, per-model breakdowns, item resampling stability, REML sensitivity checks, bootstrap CIs, and 14B scale validation. This is a substantially more comprehensive study than Mesting (2026, single benchmark) or any prior isolated-factor study.\n\n5. **The G<0.25 finding is an important indictment.** Framing current standard practice (1 prompt, 1 seed, <=200 items) as producing measurement reliability below the minimum threshold accepted in any measurement discipline is alarming and should motivate the community.\n\n6. **Clear three-claim structure with well-differentiated contributions.** Each claim maps to specific tables and sections, making the paper's logic easy to follow despite its density.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The infrastructure crossing -- the claimed methodological advance over Mesting (2026) -- yields near-null results, making the paper's novelty narrative somewhat hollow (Section 6, p.6-7).** The paper's introduction frames \"crossing infrastructure with methodology in a unified G-theory framework\" as a key gap filled. But the infrastructure x methodology interaction is <0.3%, meaning this crossing contributes essentially nothing to our understanding. The paper's three genuine findings -- the gradient, the D-study reversal, and the G<0.25 indictment -- would all emerge from a Mesting-style methodology-only design replicated across 5 benchmarks. This raises the question: is the infrastructure crossing a genuine contribution or an expensive null that consumed compute without advancing knowledge? The paper should forthrightly address this in the Discussion. The confirmatory null has value (it validates isolated-factor studies), but the paper currently oversells it relative to its import.\n\n2. **Missing critical related work weakens the positioning (Section 2).** Several directly relevant papers are absent:\n   - **Brittlebench (2026)**: Reports ~50% variance from prompt perturbations with a perturbation protocol. Directly relevant to the prompt facet and omitted from text (only Mizrahi/Sclar cited for prompt sensitivity).\n   - **ThinkingMachines (2025)**: Documents 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-invariance failure in RMSNorm/GEMM/attention kernels. Directly relevant to the seed x item interaction at T=0 and to the vLLM infrastructure discussion. This reference would strengthen the mechanistic explanation for why seed effects are non-zero even at T=0.\n   - **Messina & Scotta (2026)**: The \"background temperature\" formalization. Listed in the bibliography but cited only tangentially in the paper_framing document; the actual paper text cites them once without engaging with the T_bg framework.\n   - **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)**: Alternative variance decomposition (intent/articulation/model uncertainty). Relevant as a competing framework that would enrich the Related Work discussion.\n   - **GeneralizIT (2024)**: The Python G-theory software. If used, should be cited; if not, should be discussed as a tooling reference.\n   - **Bui et al. (2025)**: Seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22). Directly relevant to the fine-tuning G-study in Section 8.2.\n\n3. **The \"first multi-benchmark G-theory analysis\" claim needs careful qualification (Section 1, Contributions).** Bouthillier et al. (2021) performed ANOVA-based variance decomposition across multiple ML benchmarks (ImageNet, CIFAR, etc.) with multiple facets. The paper positions this as \"ANOVA, not G-theory\" -- technically correct, but ANOVA and G-theory share the same variance decomposition; the difference is D-study projections. Mesting (2026) applied G-theory to LLM evaluation on one benchmark. The paper's claim is thus \"first G-theory (not ANOVA) applied to LLM evaluation (not ML generally) across multiple benchmarks.\" This is a narrow novelty window that the paper should state more precisely. A reviewer less charitable than me would call this incremental.\n\n4. **The three-tier benchmark clustering (knowledge / commonsense / reasoning) is post-hoc and lacks external validation (Section 5).** The gradient is presented as a monotonic continuum, but the benchmark ordering (MMLU > ARC > HellaSwag > GSM8K > MATH) was chosen by the authors, presumably after observing the results. An independent measure of \"reasoning intensity\" -- such as average chain-of-thought length, number of reasoning steps, or an IRT-derived difficulty parameter -- would make the continuum principled rather than constructed. Without this, the gradient could be an artifact of the specific 5 benchmarks selected, and a 6th benchmark might not fall on the line.\n\n5. **Comparison with IRT-based approaches is superficial (Section 2, paragraph 4).** The paper claims D-study budgets are \"directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling recommendations\" (Polo et al., tinyBenchmarks) but never actually compares them. What does tinyBenchmarks recommend for MMLU -- and how does it differ from the G-theory prescription of 420 items? If they agree, G-theory is confirmatory; if they disagree, the paper must explain why. This is a missed opportunity to demonstrate G-theory's added value over an established, widely-adopted approach.\n\n6. **Zero figures is a critical presentation failure for a variance decomposition paper.** 15-19 tables and no figures. Stacked-bar variance decomposition charts are the expected field idiom (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026). The cross-benchmark gradient practically demands a visual: the 35pp monotonic decline would be immediately legible in a stacked-bar chart ordered by benchmark. D-study projection curves (G vs. facet sample size) would communicate the protocol reversal far more effectively than projection grids in tables. At a top venue with 25% acceptance rate, this is a significant competitive disadvantage.\n\n7. **The fine-tuning G-study (Section 8.2) feels architecturally disjoint.** RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA is methodologically distant from 7-14B decoder inference-time evaluation. The facets differ (learning rate and data shuffle replace prompt template and temperature), the architecture differs (encoder-only vs. decoder), and the paradigm differs (training vs. inference). The finding that \"item_id is rank-1 in fine-tuning too\" is interesting but not surprising -- it would be surprising if item difficulty did not dominate in any measurement scenario. The cross-paradigm generalization claimed is weak and should be explicitly qualified as preliminary evidence, not as a contribution on par with the main findings.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gradient robustness**: If you added a 6th benchmark (e.g., GPQA, TruthfulQA, or HumanEval), would it fall on the monotonic gradient line? What is your prediction, and what would it mean for the gradient claim if it did not?\n\n2. **Gradient mechanism**: You attribute the shift to chain-of-thought stochasticity, but HellaSwag (commonsense completion) does not typically involve explicit CoT. What mechanism drives the gradient for HellaSwag specifically?\n\n3. **IRT comparison**: For MMLU specifically, Polo et al. (2024) recommend ~100 items via IRT-based subsampling. Your D-study recommends 420 items for G>=0.90. Can you explain this 4x discrepancy? Are these answering different questions?\n\n4. **Judge variance interaction**: Mesting (2026) found judge identity dominates at 43.8%. Your entire variance structure sits \"underneath\" this. If a practitioner uses LLM-as-judge evaluation, do your D-study budgets still apply, or are they overwhelmed by judge variance?\n\n5. **Incremental over Mesting**: If Mesting had replicated their study on 5 benchmarks instead of 1, they would have discovered the same gradient (since infrastructure contributes <0.3%). What does the infrastructure crossing add beyond a confirmatory null?\n\n6. **Counter-intuitive G pattern**: You note that knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks (MMLU 0.36 vs GSM8K 0.55). Is this because the G coefficient is dominated by item sampling error on MMLU? If so, this is a feature of G-theory's definition rather than a property of the benchmarks themselves. Can you disentangle?\n\n7. **Negative variance estimates**: How are Henderson III negative estimates handled for near-zero components? This directly affects the separability claim.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography contains 22 references. The paper_framing document targets 25+. Several of the missing references listed above would bring this closer to adequate coverage for a venue submission.\n\n2. Vania et al. (2021) appears in the bibliography but is not discussed in the Related Work text.\n\n3. The title includes \"Evaluation Metrics\" (plural) but the main analysis uses only binary correct scoring. The logprob analysis is a single paragraph in Discussion. Either deliver the metric-conditional analysis promised by the title or revise the title.\n\n4. Table density: 15+ tables in 10 pages is extremely dense. Several tables (e.g., Tables 3 and 4 on per-model breakdowns) could be consolidated or moved to an appendix without losing main-text narrative.\n\n5. The claim \"over 4 million evaluation records\" in the abstract should be verified against the sum of reported records: MMLU (1,036,800) + cross-benchmark (1,382,400) + hardware (691,200) + 14B (345,600) + fine-tuning (records unclear) + MATH (460,800) + LayerCast (172,800) = ~4.1M. The accounting is plausible but should be presented explicitly.\n\n6. Section 3.3 mentions \"V0 engine in enforce_eager mode\" without defining these vLLM-specific terms for readers unfamiliar with vLLM internals.\n\n### Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Rationale**: The cross-benchmark variance gradient is a genuine empirical contribution that advances the field's understanding of LLM evaluation variance. The D-study budgets are practically useful and the G<0.25 finding is important. However, the paper's claimed methodological advance over Mesting (infrastructure crossing) yields near-null results, meaning the novelty rests primarily on replicating a known methodology across more benchmarks and discovering an empirical regularity -- valuable, but not a methodological contribution. Missing related work, zero figures, a somewhat post-hoc gradient construction, and the absence of a direct IRT comparison weaken the presentation. With figures added, missing references incorporated, and the gradient externally validated, this would move to a clear 5/6. As submitted, it sits at the acceptance threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":149194,"totalTokens":58910,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1624,"cache_read_input_tokens":54082,"output_tokens":3203,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1624},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3203,"cache_read_input_tokens":54082,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1624,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a70d5c9b395305444","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEGDQ66Ed9fQrFQxTEGNgg","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":76750,"tool_uses":29,"duration_ms":212320},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"860c10a3-5685-4534-b873-d88fdfe610ed","session_id":"7a1cd9cb-5673-4b3d-b760-963c998bdaf6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SncsxEtdyFAUZgJzrRfaLu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017jin6tikNQ6SFQYUaNFM2h","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T222113","overall_score":"weak accept","report":"## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory to LLM evaluation across five benchmarks, six models, and 4M+ records, crossing infrastructure (precision, hardware) with methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and measurement (item) facets. The revised version addresses most structural issues from Dream #1: model is now treated as a crossed facet, bootstrap CIs are provided, the bibliography is expanded to 22 references, and the cross-benchmark variance gradient is correctly positioned as the primary contribution. The experimental design is ambitious and largely sound, with internal numerical consistency verified across tables. The paper demonstrates that variance structure shifts systematically from item-dominated (MMLU 56%) to interaction-dominated (MATH 21%), and that D-study optimal allocations reverse across task types.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Internal numerical consistency is excellent.** Variance components in Table 1 sum to 100.0%. Model-as-facet Table 6 rows all sum to 100.0% (e.g., MMLU: 49.2+10.2+5.9+3.0+6.2+2.8+12.0+10.7=100.0). The interaction gradient in Table 5 is arithmetically consistent with Table 4 (e.g., MMLU sum(int*item)=18.4 matches t*i+s*i+p*i+prec*i+ord*i = 5.9+3.0+6.2+1.8+1.5=18.4). D-study projections in Tables 9-10 are internally consistent with the G_full and G_cur values in Table 7.\n\n2. **The engine confound discovery and correction is a genuine methodological contribution.** Identifying that vLLM routes FP32 through V1 CUDA graph vs V0 eager for BF16/FP16, inflating precision*item from 1.8% to 7.2% and creating an 8.5pp accuracy gap, is valuable to any researcher using vLLM. The correction (standardizing all precisions to V0 enforce_eager) is principled, and the before/after comparison (Table 1 MVP column) is convincing. The freed ~5.4% variance redistributing into residual is consistent with confound removal.\n\n3. **Model-as-facet reanalysis (Table 6) addresses the most critical methodological gap from Dream #1.** Item_id drops by ~6-7pp across all benchmarks (MMLU: 56.0->49.2, GSM8K: 25.0->22.6), with model*item absorbing ~10% on MMLU. Critically, item_id remains rank-1 on all benchmarks and the gradient is preserved (49.2->38.4->30.8->22.6). This properly attributes model heterogeneity rather than inflating item estimates.\n\n4. **Item resampling stability (Table 3) is a well-designed robustness check.** Three independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples with CV=4.1% on item_id and consistent rank ordering across samples. The 30pp margin between item_id (lowest sample: 52.8%) and the second-largest component eliminates single-sample artifact concerns.\n\n5. **The factorial design for MMLU (exp-001) is densely crossed and large-scale.** 4 models x 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings x 200 items = 1,036,800 records. This is among the largest factorial designs in the LLM evaluation variance literature. The per-model consistency (Table 2: item_id 51.6-60.2% across four models) rules out single-model artifacts.\n\n6. **The D-study facet effectiveness inversion (Table 11) is the paper's most practically useful finding.** The items:seeds effectiveness ratio shifting from 14.2:1 (MMLU) to 0.8:1 (GSM8K) has direct implications for evaluation protocol design and explains why prior studies on different benchmarks reached contradictory conclusions.\n\n7. **Bootstrap CIs on item_id (Table 8) with non-overlapping intervals across adjacent benchmarks provide statistical support for the gradient.** The MMLU-ARC gap (CI upper 52.3 vs CI lower 35.6) and ARC-HellaSwag gap (CI upper 41.2 vs CI lower 28.2) show the gradient is not an artifact of sampling variability.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n#### Major\n\n1. **Henderson Method I vs Method III discrepancy in the text.** The code (`variance_decomposition.py`) implements Henderson Method I (Type-I SS for balanced designs, solving EMS equations), but Section 3.1 (line 118) claims \"Henderson's Method III (expected mean squares)\" is used. Henderson's Method I and Method III are distinct estimation procedures -- Method I uses fitting-constants for balanced designs while Method III uses Searle's approach for unbalanced designs. For the balanced crossed design here, Method I is appropriate and the code is correct, but the paper misnames it. Table 1 caption says \"SE and 95% CI from Henderson's Method III\" -- this is incorrect given the code. Additionally, the paper later references \"REML vs Henderson\" sensitivity (Section 7.3, line 533) with <=1.4pp differences and calls this \"Henderson Method I\" -- contradicting the earlier \"Method III\" claim. This inconsistency should be resolved.\n\n2. **The D-study projection for GSM8K contains an arithmetically suspicious result.** In Table 10 (D-study GSM8K), the row \"150 items, 3 prompts, 10 seeds\" achieves G=0.90, while \"200 items, 3 prompts, 10 seeds\" achieves G=0.93. Yet in Table 7, the \"optimal\" allocation for GSM8K is listed as 150 items with 10 seeds. The issue: reducing items from 200 to 150 while achieving higher G only makes sense if item_id variance is already small enough that removing 50 items has negligible impact on item-related error. But the G formula divides item-related variance components by n_items -- so *fewer* items should yield *lower* G, not the same or higher. This suggests the \"150 items\" recommendation in Tables 7 and 9 may be from a different optimization (e.g., multi-facet joint optimization with different prompt/seed allocations) rather than a simple projection, but the paper does not explain the optimization procedure. The D-study code (`d_study.py`) sweeps one facet at a time while holding others fixed, which cannot produce the joint-optimization results in Table 9. Either the optimization procedure is undocumented, or the 150-item figure is derived from a different analysis than what the code implements.\n\n3. **The \"counter-intuitive G pattern\" explanation (Section 5.1, line 283-284) has a logical gap.** The paper claims MMLU has lower G (0.36) than GSM8K (0.55) because \"high item_id means item sampling is the measurement reliability bottleneck.\" But G is defined as sigma_tau / (sigma_tau + sigma_delta), where sigma_tau is universe-score variance (fixed-facet components only: precision, temperature, and their interaction). Looking at the code, sigma_tau for MMLU would be dominated by precision (0.3%) and temperature (1.4%) main effects -- tiny values. For GSM8K, these would also be small. The G coefficient as implemented measures the ratio of fixed-facet variance to total variance -- so higher G for GSM8K means fixed facets explain proportionally more of the reduced-error variance on GSM8K. This is not the same as \"item sampling being the bottleneck.\" The paper's narrative explanation conflates the G coefficient with measurement reliability in a way that obscures what G actually measures in this design. Specifically: in a design where the object of measurement is benchmark accuracy and the facets are prompt/seed/ordering/item, the \"universe score\" is determined by which facets are treated as fixed vs random. The paper's partition (precision and temperature as fixed) means G measures how stable results are across prompt/seed/ordering/item variations *given* a fixed precision and temperature -- not overall measurement reliability.\n\n4. **The paper claims \"4 million evaluation records\" (abstract, line 32) but the itemized totals fall short.** MMLU: 1,036,800. ARC+HellaSwag+GSM8K: 3 x 460,800 = 1,382,400. MATH: 460,800. Hardware: 691,200. 14B: 345,600. Fine-tuning: 1,728 runs (but unclear record count per run). Item resampling: 32,400. Even summing all inference experiments without double-counting (since hardware/14B experiments likely overlap with MMLU data), the total is closer to ~3.9M if MATH and hardware are fully independent, or ~3M if there is overlap. The \"over 4 million\" claim needs verification that these are truly independent records, not overlapping subsets counted multiple times.\n\n#### Moderate\n\n5. **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks are insufficiently justified.** ARC/HellaSwag/GSM8K/MATH use 2 precisions x 2 temperatures (vs 3x3 for MMLU), but the paper does not explain why. If the reduction is due to GPU budget constraints, this should be stated explicitly. More importantly, the cross-benchmark gradient (Table 4) compares variance percentages from designs with different factorial structure (MMLU: 3 precisions, 3 temperatures vs others: 2 precisions, 2 temperatures). Variance percentages are relative to total variance, which changes with the design. Removing a precision level and a temperature level changes the total variance denominator and redistributes percentage shares. The paper should discuss whether the gradient would hold if all benchmarks used the identical design.\n\n6. **Bootstrap resampling items with replacement while preserving balanced design (bootstrap_ci.py, lines 117-120) creates artificial correlation.** When a bootstrap sample draws the same item twice, the code assigns unique IDs (\"boot_0\", \"boot_1\") and treats them as independent items. But these duplicate items have identical responses across all conditions, violating the independence assumption underlying the ANOVA decomposition. This inflates the stability of item_id estimates. A cluster bootstrap (resampling items and their complete observation vectors without re-indexing) would be more appropriate, though it would produce an unbalanced design requiring REML rather than Henderson Method I.\n\n7. **The fine-tuning G-study (Section 8.2) uses a qualitatively different experimental paradigm.** RoBERTa-large is an encoder-only model fine-tuned on classification tasks; the inference experiments use decoder-only autoregressive models on generation/MC tasks. The variance hierarchy comparison (Table 13) across paradigms is informative but the claim that \"item dominance generalizes beyond inference to training\" overstates what this comparison can support. The facets themselves are different (learning rate and data shuffle replace prompt template and temperature), making direct comparison of variance percentages across paradigms questionable.\n\n8. **The paper reports G_cur (current practice) in Table 7 but the definition of \"current practice\" is not consistent.** Table 7 defines G_cur values (MMLU 0.22, GSM8K 0.14), and Table 9 shows MMLU with \"200 items, 1 prompt, 1 seed\" = G=0.22. But \"current practice\" typically includes a fixed temperature (usually 0) and a single precision -- if these are treated as fixed facets in the D-study, they should not contribute to error variance. The paper should clarify exactly what design parameters constitute \"current practice\" and whether the G_cur values assume temperature=0 or marginalize over temperature levels.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Is the estimation method Henderson Method I (as implemented in the code) or Henderson Method III (as stated in the text)? If Method I, please correct the text. If Method III, please reconcile with the code.\n\n2. How is the joint D-study optimization (Table 9) computed? The provided D-study code sweeps one facet at a time. The joint recommendations (e.g., GSM8K: 150 items + 3 prompts + 10 seeds) appear to come from a different procedure. Please describe the optimization algorithm.\n\n3. For the counter-intuitive G pattern: what exactly is sigma_tau in your D-study? If precision and temperature are treated as fixed, sigma_tau is their variance plus their mutual interaction -- all tiny values. Doesn't this mean G is measuring something quite narrow (stability across precision/temperature choices) rather than overall measurement reliability?\n\n4. Can you provide the total observation count broken down by experiment, confirming non-overlap, to support the \"4 million\" claim?\n\n5. The abstract says \"item selection is the largest single variance source on all benchmarks\" with 56% to 21%. But on MATH (Appendix A), Llama-3.1-8B shows temperature*item (21.8%) exceeding item_id (20.6%), and Mistral-7B shows temperature*item (22.8%) exceeding item_id (17.8%). How do you reconcile the \"all benchmarks\" claim when two of four models on MATH show item_id is *not* the largest source?\n\n6. What is the effective sample size of the bootstrap after accounting for duplicate items? With 200 items and replacement sampling, each bootstrap draw will have ~127 unique items on average (by the birthday problem). Does this deflation affect CI coverage?\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The abstract states the item dominance gradient as \"56% on knowledge-retrieval tasks (MMLU) down to 21% on complex reasoning (MATH), a 35 percentage-point gradient.\" But Table 1 reports 56.0% for the pooled analysis, while Table 6 (model-as-facet, which is the more principled analysis) reports 49.2% for MMLU. The abstract should use the model-as-facet figures.\n\n2. Table 1 caption refers to \"SE\" but no standard error column is visible -- the columns are % Var, SE, CI Low, CI Up, MVP. Confirm that the SE column values are present and correctly computed.\n\n3. The paper uses \"infra x method\" as a single aggregate value (0.4% in Table 1) but does not list which specific interactions are included. From the 6-facet design (precision is the only infrastructure facet without hardware), the infrastructure x methodology interactions would be: precision x prompt, precision x seed, precision x temperature, precision x ordering. With hardware added (Table 6), this expands. Please enumerate.\n\n4. Line 118 says \"Henderson's Method III\" but line 533 says \"REML vs Henderson\" with no method number specified. Pick one consistent name.\n\n5. Table 12 (14B vs 8B) shows DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B with temp*item=8.8% and seed*item=5.2%, both above the 8B range. The text attributes this to \"reasoning distillation introducing implicit chain-of-thought tendencies.\" This is speculative -- DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B is architecturally different (distilled from a reasoning model), and the elevated interaction terms could also reflect the distillation training distribution rather than implicit CoT. The causal claim should be weakened.\n\n6. No figures. The cross-benchmark gradient and D-study projection curves would be substantially more impactful as visualizations than as tables. This is a presentation weakness, not a methodological one, but at a competitive venue it matters.\n\n7. The paper does not report effect sizes or practical significance thresholds beyond the 0.3% separability value. For which variance components is the estimate \"practically significant\"? A threshold framework (e.g., components >1% are \"practically relevant\") would aid interpretation.\n\n8. Reference [7] (Hua et al. 2025) is cited but never discussed in the Related Work section -- it appears only in the design decisions. Either discuss it or remove it.\n\n### Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2239,"cache_read_input_tokens":73771,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2239,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEGDQ66Ed9fQrFQxTEGNgg","session_id":"7a1cd9cb-5673-4b3d-b760-963c998bdaf6","uuid":"d56b724a-0070-4e6f-8166-43ba634dd3a1"}
